tmux is the bus. o is the table. no plumber.

the decision

one dispatcher, bin/o, reached by every producer. one resolver, sh/c,
which sh/p asks, for text whose meaning is not yet known. ~/lib/plumbing
stops being a second table and becomes a shim into p. nothing new runs.

the drift that raised this — djvu evince against djvu zathura, mp3 mpv
against mp3 mocp — is two owners of one table, and the fix for two
owners is one owner (`general/root`). a router removes no owner; it
adds a third participant.

the bus was never vacant

inside tmux, tmux is not a sibling of ed and fm, it is what they run
inside: a long-lived user server with a namespace, addressable objects,
and a way to hand a message to one of them. that is the plumber's job,
already filled and already paid for. gio's window is the other host,
and its answer is below (revisit when).

	plumber           tmux
	the daemon        the server, running regardless
	$NAMESPACE        /tmp/tmux-$uid/{default,...}, $TMUX, -L
	ports             panes: %57, {marked}, {last}
	plumb/send        set-option -p, run-shell, send-keys
	attr name=value   pane options: @p
	wdir              #{pane_current_path}
	click             #{mouse_x}, #{mouse_word}
	plumb start       run-shell -b
	plumb client      split-window, spawn -t

`tmux/conf:/set-option -pF @p/` sets @p and `sh/p:/show $pane -pqv @p/`
reads it — a name=value pair carried out of band so screen text is
never a shell command. that is a plumb message, built for plumb's
reason, arrived at without it. `sh/p:/dst='{marked}'/` sends to
{marked}, else the frame's shell, else {last} — a port you point at and
can see, whose failure is text in the wrong prompt and not a message
into nowhere; a port found and not pointed at takes text only while a
shell waits at its prompt.

a second daemon duplicates the transport and is blind to the first
one's objects. that blindness is the cost in full: a plumber started
from the X session cannot name a pane, so plumb start e cannot split
the right one. named attributes are not rule variables — $attr is the
whole string — so the pane crosses back as text for another script to
unpick. a script to recover what tmux gives away.

the rules file cannot hold the rules

the language is is, isdir, isfile, matches, set, add, delete, and to,
client, start. no shell predicate. the commit check at
`sh/p:/git rev-parse -q --verify/`, and the a/ b/ strip and the
repo-root second guess at `sh/c:/the second guess/`, cannot be written
in it and stay in p and c. inverting does not move the decision, it
splits it: the easy half becomes rules, the hard half does not move,
and there are two owners again, one level down.

the shape

	tmux/conf   gesture    which text, which pane   needs the grid
	sh/c        resolve    text → object            needs a shell
	sh/o        dispatch   object → program         needs a name

each layer needs what its owner has and nothing else does. plumb wants
resolve and dispatch in one language and can do half of resolve. one
mechanism per thing beats three composed through a router
(`general/simple`).

what is given up

extent by pattern. plumb takes the longest leftmost match containing
the click; word-separators " " is the cheap version and it is global,
so double-click and every copy-mode word motion move with it. if it
ever bites: mouse_x and copy_cursor_x into p, matched against p's own
patterns — same idea, no daemon.
`design/address:/That one line is the enabler/` named it.

delivery to a running program with no pane. tmux can start one, never
hand a message to one already open. answered per program where it
matters — `sh/o:/mocp -S/`, mocp -S then mocp -l, is a plumb client
rule written by hand for the one program that needed it, kept in the
dispatcher. a frame's ed and fm are the other answer: they follow cur,
so a message to them is c, and the eye going to their slot
(`design/mouse:/the frame is where it lands/`).

revisit when

a gesture source outside tmux meets a consumer that must be reached
while already running. a second gesture source alone changes nothing;
it execs p, as tmux does. ed is one now: a right or a middle click there
hands its word to p (`ed/4-view/29-plumb.go`), and p takes wdir from the
pane (`sh/p:/pane_current_path/`), ed's cwd, not the edited file's
folder; a word relative to that folder misses until p takes -w. in fm
and the lens, through sys/view, a right click is the program's own
enter, and a middle one hands its word to p -a. gio's window is such a
source, and it met such a consumer, the frame's tabs: it is asked by a
file, as cur is said (`design/lens:/The tabs in gio's window/`).

-CC is a different question

control mode, the text protocol -CC runs with echo off as iTerm2 does,
was turned down here as the road to owning the grid: its client takes
raw bytes and must be a terminal, and having no tty it never fires
tmux's mouse bindings, so mouse_word, copy_cursor_word, copy-mode and
copy-pipe all go (`design/address:/Not a terminal/`). gio's window
took another road to a grid of its own: its shells run in a terminal it
holds itself, st's as sys/vt has it (`gio/term.go`), and control mode
stays turned down.
